# 3️⃣ Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree

## 📖 Definition

### Serialization

Convert a Binary Tree into a string or sequence so that it can be stored or transmitted.

### Deserialization

Convert the serialized data back into the original Binary Tree.

---

### Example

Tree:

```text
        1
       / \
      2   3
```

Possible serialization:

```text
1,2,#,#,3,#,#
```

Here:

```text
# = NULL
```

---

## 💡 Why Store NULL?

Consider:

```text
    1
   /
  2
```

and

```text
1
 \
  2
```

Without NULL markers, their traversals can become ambiguous.

NULL markers preserve the structure.

---

## 🔹 Serialization

A common approach is preorder traversal:

```text
Root
Left
Right
```

---

### C++ Code

```cpp
void serialize(Node* root, string &result)
{
    if(root == NULL)
    {
        result += "#,";
        return;
    }

    result += to_string(root->data) + ",";

    serialize(root->left, result);

    serialize(root->right, result);
}
```

---

## 🔹 Deserialization

Read values from the serialized sequence.

If the value is:

```text
#
```

return NULL.

Otherwise:

```text
Create Node

↓

Build Left Subtree

↓

Build Right Subtree
```

---

### C++ Code

```cpp
Node* deserialize(
    vector<string>& values,
    int &index)
{
    if(index >= values.size())
        return NULL;

    if(values[index] == "#")
    {
        index++;
        return NULL;
    }

    Node* root =
        new Node(stoi(values[index]));

    index++;

    root->left =
        deserialize(values, index);

    root->right =
        deserialize(values, index);

    return root;
}
```

### Complexity

```text
Time: O(n)

Space: O(n)
```

